# Where to put this (copy it, then fill in the values): the user-level file
# ~/.config/nanocosmos/.env (RECOMMENDED — survives plugin updates; same path
# on macOS, Linux and Windows/Git Bash; XDG_CONFIG_HOME is honoured; create it
# with `bash scripts/init_env.sh` from the nanostream-diagnostics skill), or
# scripts/.env next to the scripts, or the skill root as .env, or export the
# variables in your shell. NANOCOSMOS_ENV_FILE=<path> makes the scripts read
# only that file. Never commit a .env.

# Your organisation's bintu API key: Dashboard -> Organisation -> API Keys.
# Grants access to your own organisation's streams only.
# Without it (and without NANO_API_TOKEN) get_stream_config.py/.sh still
# runs against the public, keyless stream route but prints a warning; every
# other script that needs a credential exits (get_stream_info.sh and
# probe_stream_unsecure.sh need none).
# Bash scripts read this file via env.sh, Python scripts via env.py.
BINTU_API_KEY=

# Optional alternative for the read scripts, and the recommended best practice
# wherever the credential leaves your shell (services, CI, dashboards): an
# expiring ACCESS TOKEN minted with scripts/create_access_token.sh (max 365
# days). The read scripts accept it in place of the API key; minting new
# tokens still needs BINTU_API_KEY (or --from-login). The same token also
# authenticates the metrics API / analytics skill.
#NANO_API_TOKEN=

# Optional: your nanoStream dashboard login — lets create_access_token.sh
# --from-login mint an access token without any API key, and doubles as the
# analytics skill's login (same user database; ANALYTICS_EMAIL/
# ANALYTICS_PASSWORD are aliases for the same values).
#NANOSTREAM_EMAIL=
#NANOSTREAM_PASSWORD=

# Optional: path to ffprobe if it is not on PATH (used by probe_stream_*.sh)
#FFPROBE_PATH=/opt/homebrew/bin/ffprobe
